  "body": {
    "id": "prop:known-case-reduction",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "If one role is fixed in advance, or if \\(|d|\\to\\infty\\) so that one role is selected with probability tending to one, the selected limit and its symmetric-Wald coverage reduce to the fixed-role normal and nominal-coverage result; the gap-split estimator reduces as a construction to fixed-role late-block proximal inference, with its declared sample-splitting variance rather than the full-pre-period efficiency bound.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "d",
      "taugap"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "thm:selected-gaussian-limit",
      "def:gap-split-estimator",
      "lem:fixed-role-linearization"
    ],
    "justification": "The reduction verifies compatibility with the published predetermined-role theory and makes the efficiency cost of splitting explicit.",
    "gap": "The reduction is a required sanity check rather than a novelty claim.",
    "consumer": "It lets applied users interpret the correction as ordinary fixed-role proximal inference away from the selection boundary.",
    "proof_tex": "If role \\(s\\) is fixed before observing the panel, \\(\\texttt{lem:fixed-role-linearization}\\) and the Gaussian-limit premise used by \\(\\texttt{thm:selected-gaussian-limit}\\) give\n\\[\n \\sqrt n(\\widehat\\tau_s-\\tau)\\Rightarrow A_s+\\sqrt rR_s,\n \\qquad\n \\frac{\\widehat\\tau_s-\\tau}\n      {\\widehat{\\operatorname{se}}_s^{\\mathrm{conv}}}\n \\Rightarrow T_s\\sim N(0,1).\n\\]\nConsequently its symmetric Wald coverage converges to \\(2\\Phi(z)-1\\).  The same reduction occurs under diverging loss separation.  Indeed, the ridge expansion in \\(\\texttt{thm:selected-gaussian-limit}\\) shows that\n\\[\n \\sqrt n(\\widehat L_1-\\widehat L_2)\n =\\sqrt n(L_1-L_2)+D_n+o_{P_n}(1),\n \\qquad D_n=O_{P_n}(1).\n\\]\nIf \\(\\sqrt n(L_1-L_2)\\to+\\infty\\), then \\(\\Pr(\\widehat J=2)\\to1\\); if it tends to \\(-\\infty\\), then \\(\\Pr(\\widehat J=1)\\to1\\).  Substitution into the selected expansion therefore gives the corresponding fixed-role normal and nominal-coverage limit.\n\nFor the separated construction, fixing the role, or having the early selector choose it with probability tending to one, removes only the random label from \\(\\texttt{def:gap-split-estimator}\\).  The remaining operations are exactly: estimate that role's bridge on \\(\\mathcal B_n\\), average its residual on \\(\\mathcal C_n\\), and estimate the two long-run variances on those same blocks.  Since\n\\[\n |\\mathcal B_n|\n =n-\\lfloor\\varrho n\\rfloor-\\lceil(\\log n)^2\\rceil\n =(1-\\varrho)n+o(n),\n\\]\nthe fixed-role linearization on these retained blocks has, under \\(\\sqrt n\\)-normalization, variance\n\\[\n V_s^{\\mathrm{gap}}\n =\\frac{\\Omega_{\\psi,s}}{1-\\varrho}+r\\Omega_{v,s},\n\\]\nrather than the full-pre-period variance \\(V_s=\\Omega_{\\psi,s}+r\\Omega_{v,s}\\).  In fact,\n\\[\n V_s^{\\mathrm{gap}}-V_s\n =\\frac{\\varrho}{1-\\varrho}\\Omega_{\\psi,s}\\geq0,\n\\]\nwith equality only when the pre-bridge influence has zero long-run variance.  Thus the construction reduces to ordinary fixed-role late-block proximal inference with its declared split-sample variance; it does not assert recovery of the full-pre-period efficiency bound.",
